                                                    <span class="ptb-account-row-val" style="color:@a.Color;">@a.Lot2</span>
                                                </div>
                                                <div class="ptb-account-row">
                                                    <span class="ptb-account-row-label">Typical lots (2%)</span>
                                                    <span class="ptb-account-row-val" style="color:@a.Color;">@a.Lot1</span>
                                                </div>
                                            </div>
                                        </div>
                                    </div>
                                }
                            </div>
                            <p class="br-p" style="margin-top:20px;font-size:13px;color:#64748b;">Lot ranges based on EUR/USD with a 20-pip stop-loss. Actual lots vary with stop-loss distance and pip value.</p>
                        </div>

                        <div class="br-divider"></div>

                        @* Section 4 — Trading Styles *@
                        <div class="br-section" id="s4">
                            <h2 class="br-h2">4. Position Sizing by Trading Style</h2>
                            <p class="br-p">Different trading styles have very different stop-loss distances, trade frequencies, and cost structures. Your position size calculation must reflect your style — what works for a swing trader will over-risk a scalper and under-risk a position trader.</p>

                            <div class="row g-3">
                                @foreach (var t in styles)
                                {
                                    <div class="col-md-6">
                                        <div class="ptb-style-card" style="border-color:@t.Border;background:@t.Bg;">
                                            <div class="ptb-style-hd" style="color:@t.Color;">
                                                <span class="ptb-style-icon" style="background:@t.Color;"><i class="@t.Icon"></i></span>
                                                <span class="ptb-style-name">@t.Style</span>
                                            </div>
                                            <div class="ptb-style-rows">
                                                <div class="ptb-style-row">
                                                    <span>Typical Stop-Loss</span><strong>@t.SL</strong>
                                                </div>
                                                <div class="ptb-style-row">
                                                    <span>Risk per Trade</span><strong>@t.Risk</strong>
                                                </div>
                                                <div class="ptb-style-row">
                                                    <span>Trade Frequency</span><strong>@t.Trades</strong>
                                                </div>
                                            </div>
                                            <div class="ptb-style-tip">
                                                <i class="fa-solid fa-lightbulb me-1" style="color:@t.Color;"></i>@t.Tip
                                            </div>
                                        </div>
                                    </div>
                                }
                            </div>
                        </div>

                        <div class="br-divider"></div>

                        @* Section 5 — Multiple Trades *@
                        <div class="br-section" id="s5">
                            <h2 class="br-h2">5. Managing Risk Across Multiple Open Trades</h2>
                            <p class="br-p">Position sizing per trade is only half the equation. When you have multiple trades open simultaneously — especially on correlated pairs — your total portfolio risk can multiply quickly and create dangerous exposure.</p>

                            <div class="br-highlights">
                                <div class="br-highlights-title"><i class="fa-solid fa-layer-group me-2"></i>Portfolio Risk Rules</div>
                                <ul style="margin:0;padding:0 0 0 18px;line-height:2.2;font-size:14px;">
                                    <li>Keep total open risk across all trades at <strong>5–6% maximum</strong> of account balance</li>
                                    <li>EUR/USD long + GBP/USD long = <strong>double USD exposure</strong> — count as one combined position</li>
                                    <li>If you risk 1% per trade, cap at <strong>5–6 simultaneous open trades</strong> maximum</li>
                                    <li>Before adding a new position, total up the risk of all existing open stops</li>
                                    <li>During high-impact news events, consider <strong>reducing size by 50%</strong></li>
                                    <li>Correlated assets (gold + AUDUSD, EURUSD + GBPUSD) move together — treat them as one</li>
                                </ul>
                            </div>

                            <div class="br-callout">
                                <i class="fa-solid fa-triangle-exclamation me-2" style="color:#d97706;"></i>
                                <strong>Correlation warning:</strong> EUR/USD and GBP/USD have a historical correlation of 80–90%. Opening both at 1% risk each does not give you 2% diversified risk — it gives you ~1.8% of essentially the same trade. Factor correlation into your portfolio risk calculations.
                            </div>
                        </div>

                        <div class="br-divider"></div>

                        @* Section 6 — Volatility *@
                        <div class="br-section" id="s6">
                            <h2 class="br-h2">6. Adapting Position Size to Market Volatility</h2>
                            <p class="br-p">Volatility directly affects how wide your stop-loss needs to be, which changes your position size even with the same risk percentage. During high-volatility periods (major news, central bank decisions), stops must be wider to avoid being hit by normal noise — and your lot size must drop accordingly.</p>

                            <div class="br-fact-grid" style="grid-template-columns:repeat(2,1fr);margin-bottom:20px;">
                                <div class="br-fact-item">
                                    <div class="br-fact-icon" style="background:#ecfdf5;color:#059669;"><i class="fa-solid fa-water"></i></div>
                                    <div>
                                        <div class="br-fact-key">Low Volatility</div>
                                        <div class="br-fact-val">Tighter stops possible. Same risk % = more lots. Best for scalping and day trading strategies.</div>
                                    </div>
                                </div>
                                <div class="br-fact-item">
                                    <div class="br-fact-icon" style="background:#fff5f5;color:#ef4444;"><i class="fa-solid fa-tornado"></i></div>
                                    <div>
                                        <div class="br-fact-key">High Volatility</div>
                                        <div class="br-fact-val">Wider stops required. Same risk % = fewer lots. Consider reducing risk % during major news events.</div>
                                    </div>
                                </div>
                                <div class="br-fact-item">
                                    <div class="br-fact-icon" style="background:#fff7ed;color:#d97706;"><i class="fa-solid fa-calendar-days"></i></div>
                                    <div>
                                        <div class="br-fact-key">News Events</div>
                                        <div class="br-fact-val">NFP, CPI, FOMC meetings can cause 50–200 pip moves in seconds. Reduce position size by 50% or wait for the event to pass.</div>
                                    </div>
                                </div>
                                <div class="br-fact-item">
                                    <div class="br-fact-icon" style="background:#eff6ff;color:#2563eb;"><i class="fa-solid fa-moon"></i></div>
                                    <div>
                                        <div class="br-fact-key">Asian Session</div>
                                        <div class="br-fact-val">Lower volatility, tighter ranges. Scalpers can use tighter stops — but pip value and spreads may be wider on exotic pairs.</div>
                                    </div>
                                </div>
                            </div>
                        </div>

                        <div class="br-divider"></div>

                        @* Section 7 — Spread & Commission *@
                        <div class="br-section" id="s7">
                            <h2 class="br-h2">7. Including Spread &amp; Commission in Your Calculation</h2>
                            <p class="br-p">Most position size calculators only account for the stop-loss distance in pips. But your true risk also includes the spread you pay on entry and — on ECN accounts — the commission per lot. Ignoring these means you are systematically under-calculating your real risk.</p>

                            <div class="psc-formula" style="margin:20px 0;">
                                <div class="psc-formula-title">True Risk = Nominal Risk + Spread Cost + Commission</div>
                                <div class="psc-formula-eq">
                                    <span class="psc-formula-result">True Risk ($)</span>
                                    <span class="psc-formula-equals">=</span>
                                    <span class="psc-formula-fraction">
                                        <span class="psc-formula-num">SL pips + Spread pips</span>
                                        <span class="psc-formula-bar"></span>
                                        <span class="psc-formula-den">pip value per lot</span>
                                    </span>
                                    <span class="psc-formula-equals" style="color:#c4b5fd;">+</span>
                                    <span class="psc-formula-result" style="color:#c4b5fd;">Commission</span>
                                </div>
                            </div>

                            <div class="br-callout">
                                <i class="fa-solid fa-lightbulb me-2" style="color:#d97706;"></i>
                                <strong>Practical tip:</strong> On a 20-pip stop-loss with a 1.5-pip spread, your effective stop is 21.5 pips. On ECN with $7/lot commission and 0.50 lots, add $3.50 to your dollar risk. Over hundreds of trades, this difference is meaningful.
                            </div>

                            <p class="br-p">This is also why ECN accounts with cashback rebates can be more cost-effective than standard accounts with "no commission" — the cashback recovered per lot often exceeds the apparent saving from a zero-commission account.</p>
                        </div>

                        <div class="br-divider"></div>

                        @* Section 8 — Drawdown Recovery *@
                        <div class="br-section" id="s8">
                            <h2 class="br-h2">8. Recovering from a Drawdown Safely</h2>
                            <p class="br-p">Every trader — even the best — goes through losing streaks. The position size calculator is your most important tool during drawdown because it automatically reduces your lot size as your balance decreases, protecting you from a death spiral of increasing losses.</p>

                            <table class="br-facts-table">
                                <thead>
                                    <tr>
                                        <th>Account Balance</th>
                                        <th style="text-align:center;">Risk at 1%</th>
                                        <th style="text-align:center;">Lot Size (20-pip SL)</th>
                                        <th style="text-align:center;">Drawdown So Far</th>
                                    </tr>
                                </thead>
                                <tbody>
                                    <tr>
                                        <td><strong>$10,000</strong> <span style="font-size:11px;color:#16a34a;">(starting)</span></td>
                                        <td style="text-align:center;">$100</td>
                                        <td style="text-align:center;font-weight:700;">0.50 lots</td>
                                        <td style="text-align:center;color:#16a34a;">0%</td>
                                    </tr>
                                    <tr>
                                        <td><strong>$9,000</strong></td>
                                        <td style="text-align:center;">$90</td>
                                        <td style="text-align:center;font-weight:700;">0.45 lots</td>
                                        <td style="text-align:center;color:#d97706;">−10%</td>
                                    </tr>
                                    <tr>
                                        <td><strong>$8,000</strong></td>
                                        <td style="text-align:center;">$80</td>
                                        <td style="text-align:center;font-weight:700;">0.40 lots</td>
                                        <td style="text-align:center;color:#d97706;">−20%</td>
                                    </tr>
                                    <tr>
                                        <td><strong>$7,000</strong></td>
                                        <td style="text-align:center;">$70</td>
                                        <td style="text-align:center;font-weight:700;">0.35 lots</td>
                                        <td style="text-align:center;color:#ef4444;">−30%</td>
                                    </tr>
                                    <tr>
                                        <td><strong>$5,000</strong></td>
                                        <td style="text-align:center;">$50</td>
                                        <td style="text-align:center;font-weight:700;">0.25 lots</td>
                                        <td style="text-align:center;color:#ef4444;">−50%</td>
                                    </tr>
                                </tbody>
                            </table>
                            <p class="br-p" style="font-size:13px;color:#64748b;margin-top:-20px;">With fixed-risk % sizing, each loss reduces your lot size — slowing the drawdown automatically. With fixed lot sizing, each loss hits equally hard regardless of your reduced balance.</p>
                        </div>

                        <div class="br-divider"></div>

                        @* Section 9 — Cashback *@
                        <div class="br-section" id="s9">
                            <h2 class="br-h2">9. Cashback — Lower Your Effective Trading Cost</h2>
                            <p class="br-p">Every trade has a cost — the spread, commission, and swap. Cashback rebates paid per lot traded directly offset these costs, reducing your effective risk on every position and improving your net P&amp;L without changing your strategy.</p>

                            <div class="br-highlights">
                                <div class="br-highlights-title"><i class="fa-solid fa-circle-dollar-to-slot me-2"></i>How Cashback Improves Your Position Sizing Math</div>
                                <ul style="margin:0;padding:0 0 0 18px;line-height:2.2;font-size:14px;">
                                    <li>You risk $100 on a 0.50-lot EUR/USD trade</li>
                                    <li>Cashback received: <strong>0.50 lots × $7.50 = $3.75</strong></li>
                                    <li>Net risk after cashback: <strong>$100 − $3.75 = $96.25</strong></li>
                                    <li>You effectively pay the spread only 96.25% of what you thought</li>
                                    <li>Over 200 trades per month: <strong>$750/month returned in cashback</strong></li>
                                    <li>That is equivalent to recovering the spread on every single trade</li>
                                </ul>
                            </div>

                            <p class="br-p">For scalpers and high-frequency traders, cashback becomes a major component of overall profitability. Even for swing traders taking a few trades per week, the annual cashback on standard lot sizes is a meaningful reduction in net trading cost.</p>

                            <div class="row g-3 mt-1">
                                <div class="col-sm-4">
                                    <a asp-controller="Home" asp-action="ForexCaculator" class="frt-tool-btn">
                                        <i class="fa-solid fa-calculator frt-tool-btn-icon" style="color:#7c3aed;"></i>
                                        <span class="frt-tool-btn-label">Cashback Calculator</span>
                                        <i class="fa-solid fa-arrow-right frt-tool-btn-arrow"></i>
                                    </a>
                                </div>
                                <div class="col-sm-4">
                                    <a asp-controller="Resources" asp-action="BestCashbackBrokers" class="frt-tool-btn">
                                        <i class="fa-solid fa-trophy frt-tool-btn-icon" style="color:#d97706;"></i>
                                        <span class="frt-tool-btn-label">Best Cashback Brokers</span>
                                        <i class="fa-solid fa-arrow-right frt-tool-btn-arrow"></i>
                                    </a>
                                </div>
                                <div class="col-sm-4">
                                    <a asp-controller="Resources" asp-action="HowToChooseBroker" class="frt-tool-btn">
                                        <i class="fa-solid fa-compass frt-tool-btn-icon" style="color:#16a34a;"></i>
                                        <span class="frt-tool-btn-label">How to Choose a Broker</span>
                                        <i class="fa-solid fa-arrow-right frt-tool-btn-arrow"></i>
                                    </a>
                                </div>
                            </div>
                        </div>

                        <div class="br-divider"></div>

                        @* Section 10 — Risk % Questions *@
                        <div class="br-section" id="s10">
                            <h2 class="br-h2">10. Common Questions About Risk Percentage</h2>

                            <div class="br-fact-grid" style="grid-template-columns:repeat(2,1fr);margin-bottom:0;">
                                <div class="br-fact-item">
                                    <div class="br-fact-icon" style="background:#faf5ff;color:#7c3aed;"><i class="fa-solid fa-question"></i></div>
                                    <div>
                                        <div class="br-fact-key">Is 2% too high?</div>
                                        <div class="br-fact-val">For beginners: yes. Start at 0.5–1%. Only increase to 2% when you have a proven track record over 50+ trades.</div>
                                    </div>
                                </div>
                                <div class="br-fact-item">
                                    <div class="br-fact-icon" style="background:#faf5ff;color:#7c3aed;"><i class="fa-solid fa-question"></i></div>
                                    <div>
                                        <div class="br-fact-key">Can I risk more on "A+" setups?</div>
                                        <div class="br-fact-val">Not recommended. Even the best setups fail. Consistent 1% risk is statistically superior to variable sizing over hundreds of trades.</div>
                                    </div>
                                </div>
                                <div class="br-fact-item">
                                    <div class="br-fact-icon" style="background:#faf5ff;color:#7c3aed;"><i class="fa-solid fa-question"></i></div>
                                    <div>
                                        <div class="br-fact-key">Should risk % change during drawdown?</div>
                                        <div class="br-fact-val">Some traders reduce to 0.5% during a drawdown to slow the decline. Never increase risk percentage to "make back losses faster".</div>
                                    </div>
                                </div>
                                <div class="br-fact-item">
                                    <div class="br-fact-icon" style="background:#faf5ff;color:#7c3aed;"><i class="fa-solid fa-question"></i></div>
                                    <div>
                                        <div class="br-fact-key">Does funded account sizing differ?</div>
                                        <div class="br-fact-val">Yes. Prop firm accounts typically cap daily drawdown at 4–5% total. Risk 0.5–1% per trade and stay well within the daily drawdown limit.</div>
                                    </div>
                                </div>
                            </div>
                        </div>

                        <div class="br-divider"></div>

                        @* FAQs *@
                        <div class="br-section" id="s11">
                            <h2 class="br-h2">11. Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
                            <div class="br-faq">
                                @foreach (var faq in faqs)
                                {
                                    var uid = Guid.NewGuid().ToString("N")[..6];
                                    <div class="br-faq-item">
                                        <button class="br-faq-btn collapsed" type="button"
                                                data-bs-toggle="collapse" data-bs-target="#faq-@uid"
                                                aria-expanded="false">
                                            @faq.Q
                                            <i class="fa-solid fa-chevron-down ms-auto"></i>
                                        </button>
                                        <div id="faq-@uid" class="collapse">
                                            <div class="br-faq-body">@faq.A</div>
                                        </div>
                                    </div>
                                }
                            </div>
                        </div>

                        @* Bottom CTA *@
                        <div class="br-bottom-cta">
                            <div style="font-size:12px;font-weight:700;color:#7c3aed;letter-spacing:.1em;text-transform:uppercase;margin-bottom:10px;">Take Control of Your Risk</div>
                            <h2 style="font-size:clamp(20px,3vw,28px);font-weight:900;color:#0f1b2d;margin:0 0 10px;">Size Every Trade Right. Earn Cashback. Trade Smarter.</h2>
                            <p style="font-size:14px;color:#64748b;max-width:560px;margin:0 auto 24px;line-height:1.7;">Position sizing is free. Cashback is free. Both improve your trading results immediately — without changing your strategy at all.</p>
                            <div class="br-cta-btns">
                                <a asp-controller="Resources" asp-action="PositionSizeCalculator" class="br-cta-btn-primary" style="background:linear-gradient(135deg,#7c3aed,#6d28d9);">
                                    <i class="fa-solid fa-calculator me-2"></i>View Formulas &amp; Examples
                                </a>
                                <a asp-controller="Home" asp-action="ForexCaculator" class="br-cta-btn-ghost">
                                    <i class="fa-solid fa-circle-dollar-to-slot me-2"></i>Cashback Calculator
                                </a>
                            </div>
                        </div>

                    </article>
                </div>

